Chromatography and mass spectra supply complementary evidence (@cm-residue-chromatography: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../../../chemistry/general-chemistry/sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T", #source "../sources/Nonvolatile-Residue-Collection-and-Analysis-NASA.lingua#r_2YMCRSWR07NCHECM0X4D8FJ6HJ", #clean-manufacturing "An-internal-standard-and-standard-addition-answer-different-questions.lingua#r_1SMQ8FT113SR7EW4KHN52FYBZZ") { r_2QKJC6Z59PH7NK3G5Y0N9TS23K
**Read:** [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../../../chemistry/general-chemistry/sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), chapter 12 and the relevant spectroscopic discussion in chapter 10; [Nonvolatile residue collection and analysis — Gordon, Rutherford and Aranda](../sources/Nonvolatile-Residue-Collection-and-Analysis-NASA.lingua#r_2YMCRSWR07NCHECM0X4D8FJ6HJ), GCMS interpretation and analytical examples.

Chromatography separates material according to interactions and transport under a defined method. A detector then responds to what reaches it. Retention time, peak shape and mass-to-charge signals are observations with conditions, not self-sufficient identities.

Two compounds can overlap in retention time, and a fragment can be shared by different compounds. Preparation or inlet conditions can also change what reaches the detector. A library match supplies a candidate to test against references, blanks and alternative explanations.

Using baseline peak widths w₁,w₂, a conventional resolution measure is Rs = 2(t₂ − t₁)/(w₁ + w₂). For retention times 5 and 5.4 min and widths 0.3 and 0.4 min, Rs ≈ 1.142857. This summarizes separation in the stated convention; it does not guarantee a correct identification or unbiased component areas for every peak shape.

**Work:** Compare two synthetic overlapping peaks with different detector-response factors. Show how a small component can be obscured by a larger one and how a second spectral channel can help. Separate confirmation of identity, quantitative calibration and a tentative source attribution. The NASA example's strong identification language should be evaluated against the actual evidence available for the specimen.

